    {
     "tipo": "explicacion",
     "titulo": "Couples and children",
     "texto": [
      "Today we talk about couples, families and children.\n\n- **Couples:** two people can **casarse** (get married, with a **boda**) and then go on a **luna de miel**. If the relationship ends, they **se separan** and, later, there may be a **divorcio**.\n- **Children:** parents can **adoptar** a child. You say **educar** a son or daughter. The youngest go to the **guardería**, and at night a **canguro** can look after them.\n- **Behaviour:** a child can **portarse bien** and **obedecer** their parents. A child who plays small pranks is **travieso**; one who is disrespectful is **maleducado**.\n- **Careful:** **educado** means «polite and courteous», not «with an education».\n- **Time together:** **llevar** + time: llevan diez años juntos (they have been together for ten years)."
     ],
     "tutora": ""
    },
    {
     "tipo": "gramatica",
     "titulo": "Llevar + time",
     "texto": [
      "To say **for how long** something has lasted, Spanish uses **llevar** + time:\n\n- **llevar** + time + adjective or adverb: **llevan diez años juntos** (they have been together for ten years).\n- **llevar** + time + gerund: **llevo dos años viviendo aquí** (I have been living here for two years).\n\nThe verb **llevar** changes with the person (llevo, llevas, lleva, llevamos, lleváis, llevan); the time it counts does not. It is a very natural way to talk about couples, jobs and habits."
     ],
